# Item index — closed items (`iN` registry, closed half)

This is the **resolved half** of the canonical, milestone-neutral `iN` registry.
Every row here carries a `DONE` or `WONTFIX` status token — it has been completed
or deliberately declined. Ids are **globally unique** with `item-registry-open.md`
and are never reused.

Governance, open backlog, and the shared registry discipline live in
`docs/notes/item-registry-open.md` (link it).

| id | item | status | pointer |
|----|------|--------|---------|
| **i91** | Phase-3 netboot **host oracle harness** (`tools/netboot-oracle/`, new Go module — 2026-06-14, netboot impl plan §8.1 foundation). The pre-i80 verification net for the netboot protocol logic: pure-Go DHCP + TFTP packet builders/parsers (the byte-for-byte authority for the Z80 i82 client / i83 server / i86 DHCP responder) replayed against **masked golden vectors** extracted from Pete's real Pi 400 capture (`~/tftp-logs`, off-repo). Packages: `frame` (the §1.2 Ethernet/IPv4/UDP offset contract + `BuildUDPFrame`, the Go authority for the Z80 `build_udp_frame` fresh-frame primitive trinload lacks), `dhcp` (OFFER/ACK builder + the fixed option-43 "Raspberry Pi Boot" blob), `tftp` (RRQ/OACK/DATA/ACK/ERROR + serve-by-name resolve: 404 serial-subdir, ERROR(1)-on-miss-keep-serving + the client/server transfer-loop state machines: SAS retransmit fix, streamed send, short-final-block termination), `pcap` (dependency-free libpcap/pcapng reader), `golden` (committed masked vectors via `cmd/gen-golden`). Wired into CI as the `netboot-oracle` pure-Go job + `STATICCHECK_MODULES`. **Validates protocol logic only** — the Z80 execution, ENC28J60 I/O, and end-to-end Pi boot are NOT host-verifiable (gated on i80/real-Trinity). | DONE — ✅ **DONE** (PR #219) — module + golden vectors + 17 oracle tests green (incl. `BuildUDPFrame` round-trip + byte-exact RRQ/OACK builders vs the capture + the client/server transfer-loop state machines); CI-gated; no off-repo captures committed (real MACs/IPs/serials masked to documentation placeholders, option-43 blob byte-identical) | `tools/netboot-oracle/`; `docs/plans/phase3-netboot-implementation-plan.md` §6/§8.1; `docs/notes/pi-netboot-capture-analysis.md`; i82, i83, i86, i80 |
| **i94** | Phase-3 netboot **bring-up smoke test** — the first *bootable, runnable* netboot program (the components from i91/i92/i80/i93 were verified pieces; this is the first one Pete can boot on real Trinity). `src/netboot/smoke_test.asm`: a tiny program that runs `drv_init` on the real Trinity then loops answering **ARP requests for the SAM's IP** with an ARP reply — one observable network action confirming the Ethernet path comes up and talks (the "hello, the wire works" before the full server). New `build_arp_reply.asm` primitive (the inverse of `build_arp_request`; the port of the new Go authority `frame/frame.go::BuildARPReply`) + a new `smoke/smoke.go::Responder` Go authority. The bootable program reads the SAM's MAC + IP from the Trinity EEPROM "Trinity Network " chunk via the vendored `eeprom.asm` (Colin Piggot, simonowen/trinload, verbatim). Ships a **bootable SAM disk** (`make netboot-smoke-disk` → B-DOS boot + AUTO that LOADs+CALLs the program at &8000) built by a new `build-disk -netboot` mode, plus the "how to test on real Trinity" guide. **Verification (the honesty line, CLAUDE.md §5):** `smoke_serve_once` IS host-verified end-to-end over the i80 emulation — inject an ARP request, the real `encdrv.asm` + `build_arp_reply` run, and the reply on the virtual wire is byte-for-byte `smoke.Responder.OnFrame` (`smoke_test_test.go`, 5 cases incl. wrong-IP/non-ARP/empty-wire controls; `make ci-netboot-z80`). NOT host-verifiable: the EEPROM config read, the real ENC28J60 TX/RX silicon timing, and the actual ARP round trip with Pete's machine — gated on real Trinity (`smoke_main` + `eeprom.asm`). Emulation-verified ≠ hardware-verified. | DONE — ✅ **DONE** (this PR) — smoke program + ARP-reply primitive + Go authority + bootable disk + testing guide, all host-verified green + CI-gated; the on-hardware ARP round trip is Pete's test (`docs/notes/netboot-trinity-testing.md` §"Increment 1"). | `src/netboot/smoke_test.asm`; `src/netboot/build_arp_reply.asm`; `src/netboot/eeprom.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/smoke/smoke.go`; `tools/netboot-oracle/frame/frame.go`; `docs/notes/netboot-trinity-testing.md`; `tools/build-disk/main.go`; i80, i82, i83, i86, i91, i92, i93 |
| **i96** | Phase-3 **serve-files TFTP demo** — a focused, bootable "serve a few files to a plain TFTP client" demo server, distinct from the i95 Pi-netboot server: **ARP + TFTP only, no DHCP and no Pi PXE option-43 blob** — so it is testable from any machine with a stock `tftp`/`curl` client (no Raspberry Pi, no DHCP setup). Pete asked for it directly (2026-06-14): bake a couple of small demo files in, boot → it serves, Pete `tftp get`s them. **LANDED (this PR):** Go authority `tools/netboot-oracle/serve/serve.go::Responder.OnFrame` (ARP request for our IP → ARP reply; UDP:69 RRQ → serve-by-name; UDP:our-TID ACK → next DATA) + the Z80 port `src/netboot/netboot_serve.asm`'s `serve_serve_once` — a *new* single-dispatch state machine composing the host-verified `build_udp_frame`/`build_arp_reply`/`tftp_build`/`tftp_parse` + the real `encdrv.asm` directly (NOT `include`ing the standalone loops — symbol collisions). The **one behaviour beyond i95:** a bare RRQ with no options (what a classic `tftp get` sends) is answered per **RFC 2347** with **DATA block 1 directly** at the 512-byte default (no OACK); an optioned RRQ (e.g. `curl`'s `tsize`) takes the OACK path. Implemented by factoring `tftp.ServerLoop.StartTransfer(frame, sendOACK)` out of `OnRRQ` (the existing OACK path stays byte-identical — i83/i95 tests regress green) and a `PARSE_OPT_COUNT==0` branch in the Z80 `handle_rrq`. Host-verified over the i80 emulation byte-for-byte vs the Go authority (`netboot_serve_test.go`: ARP reply + bare-RRQ→DATA + optioned-RRQ→OACK + miss→ERROR(1)-keep-serving; `make ci-netboot-z80`). **Bootable disk shipped** (`make netboot-serve-disk` → B-DOS boot + AUTO LOADs+CALLs `serve_main` at &8000, which reads the EEPROM MAC/IP + provisions the baked-in `hello.txt`/`readme.txt` into STORE/SRC_TABLE) + the on-hardware guide (`netboot-trinity-testing.md` §"Serve-files demo"). The demo files are assembled into the binary (no B-DOS provisioning needed — that is what makes it plain-client-testable). **Verification line (CLAUDE.md §5):** the dispatch + the bare/optioned RRQ paths ARE host-verifiable; the real ENC28J60 silicon + a stock TFTP client interoperating + the on-hardware run stay Pete's real-Trinity test. Emulation-verified ≠ hardware-verified. | DONE — ✅ DONE — Go authority + Z80 loop + bootable disk + host tests landed; on-hardware run is Pete's. | `tools/netboot-oracle/serve/serve.go`; `src/netboot/netboot_serve.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/netboot_serve_test.go`; `docs/notes/netboot-trinity-testing.md`; i80, i83, i94, i95 |
| **i97** | **Deterministic autonomous-loop monitor** — keep ONE interactive Claude Code session (in `screen`, with `/remote-control`) working autonomously across context limits, **losslessly** (never the lossy auto-compaction). A small deterministic watcher script + a *thin* protocol addendum the startup prompt points to; the proven `docs/ROADMAP.md`-driven orchestrator prompt/behaviour stays **unchanged** — no LLM supervisor, no headless `claude -p`. **Protocol (settled with Pete 2026-06-15):** the agent does one work item → writes a *task-done* semaphore → idles; the watcher injects the context readout (`/context`) **then a short resume nudge** (the nudge is load-bearing — `/context` is a *local* command that renders but does **not** invoke the model, so a real text prompt is required to wake the agent for its next turn; live-test finding, q14 failure mode 3); the agent then reads the readout and — if **<20% free** → **winds down** (writes the ROADMAP handover + a *wound-down* semaphore), else **picks the next item**; on *wound-down* the watcher stuffs **`/clear`** + the startup prompt → a genuine clean reset re-grounded from ROADMAP. Plus a **hang timeout** (the residual watchdog: no semaphore within T min while idle → nudge/escalate). **Why:** the sequencing / watchdog / clear-and-reprompt is deterministic plumbing — a script does it, not a model, which also keeps interactivity + `/remote-control` intact (a headless process would lose both). **Open before trusting overnight:** live-test that a *watcher-stuffed* `/context` reaches the agent the same way a hand-typed one does (proven hand-typed this session; same input path, expected identical). Per-task overshoot is bounded by the orchestrator delegating heavy work to subagents (its own context grows slowly) + the 20% reserve doubling as the overshoot cushion. **Screen targeting (Pete's setup, load-bearing):** his `screen` session has **multiple windows** (he opens shells with `ctrl-a c`); claude runs in the **first window** (window 0), launched via the `cl` alias. `screen -X stuff` defaults to the *active* window, so the watcher MUST target window 0 explicitly — `screen -S <session> -p 0 -X stuff "…"` — or keystrokes land in the wrong shell. | DONE — 🔨 **core BUILT** (this PR) — `tools/autonomous-loop/monitor.sh` + README; live test confirmed (q14). | `tools/autonomous-loop/`; `docs/ROADMAP.md` handover contract; `CLAUDE.md`; memory `feedback_autonomous_loop` + `feedback_implementation_autonomy` + `feedback_overnight_agent_orchestration`; q14 |
| **i98** | **Split the `iN`/`qN` registries by state** (Pete's idea, 2026-06-15) — restructure the item + question registries so an agent that wants only the *open* work needn't read the entire all-time history of every item ever created. The design-with-Pete session it was gated on happened (2026-06-15) and produced the agreed shape, executed as **i105** (open/closed split + controlled status vocabulary + atomic items). | DONE — superseded by i105 (this PR is i105); the "design WITH Pete in a dedicated session" gate it was waiting on is satisfied. | i105; `docs/notes/item-registry-open.md`; `docs/notes/item-registry-closed.md` |
| **i99** | **Stream the netboot HTTP fetch body to storage (multi-MB firmware)** — surfaced by the i70 brick-6 review (2026-06-15). The i70 HTTP fetch currently **buffers the whole response in RAM**: `tcp_conn.asm`'s `conn_accumulate` appends into a fixed **4096-byte `CONN_DATA`** (with **no bound check** — a >4 KB body overruns it), and `netboot_http.asm`'s `http_main` then copies the body into a **16 KB `STAGING`** buffer before one HSAVE. That is fine for the host tests + small files, but a real Pi firmware blob (`start4.elf` ≈ 3 MB; the `.deb` is several MB) **cannot** fit in the SAM's RAM, let alone a 4 KB/16 KB buffer. The real fetch must **stream**: write each block of body to Trinity storage **incrementally** as it arrives (append to a B-DOS record / span records via the `bdos_seam`/HSAVE-append surface) rather than accumulate-then-HSAVE, and `conn_accumulate` needs either a bound/flush hook or to be bypassed by the streaming path. **DESIGN PASS DONE (2026-06-15):** split into a host-verifiable RAM-side (autonomous-eligible NOW) + a hardware-gated B-DOS binding (a genuine Pete-decision = **q16**). **RAM-side (build now, behind an abstract sink):** convert `conn_accumulate` into a bounded `FLUSH_WINDOW` buffer that calls a `storage_sink_flush` on threshold + at the FIN, never exceeding the window in RAM (deletes the 4 KB/16 KB buffers + the overrun bug); **skip the HTTP header** before the first flush (the first window is "header+body-start"; `FLUSH_WINDOW` ≥ max header → no straddle case); **back-pressure = flush-before-ACK** (the receive loop is single-threaded `drv_read`→process→ACK, so flushing before `conn_build_and_send` withholds the ACK for free — no window arithmetic; an advertised-window shrink is a noted future-only refinement). Mirror the change in the Go authority (`tcp.Conn`/`Fetcher` route to a `Store` sink recording chunks) so the byte-for-byte oracle still holds; **host-verify** by streaming a multi-window body across ≥3 segments with a small `FLUSH_WINDOW` (≥2 flushes), a non-window-multiple length (partial-final), + the header-skip — asserting the sink's chunks are ordered/sized right + concatenate to the body (the streaming analogue of `tcp_conn_test`'s accumulated-body assert). **Hardware-gated (behind `NETBOOT_HOSTTEST`, q16-bound):** the real B-DOS record/sector write (`RST 8`), section-C paging, the end-to-end multi-MB fetch. **The B-DOS binding is q16** (the SAMDOS-2 audit says the append hooks `HOFLE`/`HSBYT` are broken for external callers, contradicting the fork analysis — Pete's call; agent leans the bounded-HSAVE-per-record convention). Not on the daily-loop critical path; blocks a *real* (vs toy-sized) on-hardware fetch. **FOUNDATION LANDED (2026-06-15):** the Go-authority streaming sink (`tcp.Sink`/`ChunkSink` + `Conn.SetSink`/`acceptPayload`/`flushFinal`, opt-in; nil sink = legacy) shipped in **PR #263**, and the **Z80 port** now mirrors it in `tcp_conn.asm` — an opt-in `CONN_SINK_ENABLED` flag (default 0 = byte-identical legacy `CONN_DATA` accumulate) that, when set, routes payload through a bounded `CONN_FLUSH_WINDOW`/`CONN_FLUSH_BUF`, flushing full windows to `storage_sink_flush` as it fills and the partial remainder at the FIN (`conn_flush_final`, before the FIN consumes its seq). `storage_sink_flush` is a recording test double (`CONN_SINK_OUT`/`CONN_SINK_CHUNKS`); host-verified by `tcp_conn_stream_test.go` (multi-window across straddling segments, ≥2 flushes, partial-final + FIN-flush, chunk boundaries cross-checked vs the Go `ChunkSink`), and the wire frames stay byte-for-byte the Go authority's (every existing oracle test green → the default path is unchanged). **q16 RESOLVED (Pete 2026-06-15): option (c)** — persist as a sequence of bounded files **spanning multiple Trinity records/disks** (~800 KB each; a multi-MB blob spans several), each a plain `HSAVE` (the *verified* hook), reassembled in order at serve time — **sidesteps the broken append hooks** (no `HOFLE`/`HSBYT`). **Correction (Pete):** the earlier "HSAVE caps at 64 KB" was WRONG — the UIFA size is pages-count×16384 + len-mod-16K (a **32-bit** size, ~4 MB/file max); the real limits are **RAM** (the source → stream) + **per-record capacity** (~800 KB → span records), not a 64 KB file ceiling (64 KB was a conflation with the 16-bit window / i24's assembler `OUT_LEN`). So the streaming window binds to a RAM-bounded chunk (the HSAVE source must be contiguous in RAM, so the per-flush chunk is bounded by free RAM — comfortably within a section-C window — NOT by the ~800 KB record/floppy capacity; "spanning records" = a sequence of bounded HSAVE'd files reassembled at serve time), each flushed as one bounded `HSAVE`. **Progress (2026-06-15):** the header-skip **`bodySink` Z80 port landed** (#279 — `src/netboot/body_sink.asm` vs `http.NewBodySink`); the **per-file `FW_MANIFEST` size landed** (the split count = size ÷ per-record-cap needs the size — now a manifest field, host-verified vs `http.RPiFirmware.Files[i].Size`). **Spanning convention — Go authority landed (2026-06-15):** `bdos.SpanPlan(name, size, cap) []SpanRecord{Name, Offset, Length}` (+ `SpanCount`, `SpanRecordName`) — the split + record-naming convention, the same plan for both the persist write (HSAVE each) and the serve read order (HLOAD each in order, concatenated). A non-spanned object (size ≤ cap) is a single record under its **plain** name (the kernel + small files keep their natural TFTP name through the existing flat store); a spanned object is `N = ceil(size/cap)` records named `<prefix7><NNN>`, the count derived from the **known size** (the manifest carries it) so **no on-disk index record is needed**. The per-record cap is **RAM-bounded** (the HSAVE source must be contiguous in paged RAM) and stays a **parameter** (pinned at hardware-build time — no speculative constant). Host-verified in `bdos/span_test.go` (boundaries, the real firmware sizes, + the reassembly invariants: records cover `[0,size)` contiguously, each ≤ cap, count == `SpanCount`, names == `SpanRecordName(0..N-1)`); spec §8 records the convention. **Z80 spanning primitives landed (2026-06-15):** `src/netboot/fw_span.asm` — `fw_span_chunk_len` (the per-record byte count `FW_SPAN_LEN = min(cap, remaining)`, 32-bit, the streaming-persist primitive; the record count falls out of the loop so no 32-bit divide) + `fw_span_record_name` (`<prefix7><NNN>`). Standalone leaf, bootable image byte-identical (`&FA52`); host-verified vs `bdos.SpanPlan`/`SpanRecordName` (`fw_span_test.go`: the streaming chunk-length sequence + the record names match byte-for-byte across non-spanned/spanned/exact-multiple/real-firmware sizes + indices to 999); `make netboot-fw-span`, in the `netboot-z80` CI job. With this, the **host-verifiable half** of the spanning convention is complete on both sides (Go authority + Z80 port). **Remaining (all hardware-gated):** the `http_main` migration onto the streaming sink (drives `fw_span_chunk_len` → HSAVE each record under `fw_span_record_name`), the serve-time reassembly read loop (HLOAD `<prefix>000..(N-1)` in order, `N` from the manifest size), and the real `RST 8` `HSAVE`/`HLOAD`-per-record — validated only on real Trinity (Pete's test). | DONE — ✅ host-verifiable half COMPLETE (#263 Go sink + Z80 port; q16 RESOLVED; bodySink #279; manifest size #281; spanning Go authority #282 + the Z80 `fw_span` port); remaining = the hardware-gated `http_main` migration / serve reassembly / real HSAVE-HLOAD (Pete's real-Trinity test) | `src/netboot/netboot_http.asm`; `src/netboot/tcp_conn.asm` (`conn_accumulate`/`CONN_WINDOW`); `src/netboot/fw_span.asm` (`fw_span_chunk_len`/`fw_span_record_name`); `src/netboot/bdos_seam.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/{tcp/conn.go,bdos/bdos.go,bdos/span.go}`; `docs/notes/sam-stub-audit.md`; `docs/specs/phase3-delivery-design.md` §8; i70, i93, i24, q15, q16 |
| **i105** | **Registry restructure — atomic items, status vocabulary, open/closed split** (Pete + agent, 2026-06-15). The item/question tables have grown large and their status cells drifted from a parseable token (early items: `✅ DONE (PR #N)`) into multi-part descriptive prose (e.g. i76's `P1a/P1b…`), so a startup agent must ingest the whole history and can't quickly answer "which items are open?"; worse, bundled multi-deliverable rows hide incomplete sub-parts (the SimCoupé PNG-endian fix sat unnoticed inside i81's prose). **Fix (agreed shape):** **(a)** split each registry into **`item-registry-open.md` + `item-registry-closed.md`** (and likewise for questions) — a fresh agent reads only the open file; resolving an item *moves* its row open→closed in the *same PR* that resolves it (mirrors "delete the plan in the completing PR"); ids stay globally unique across both files. **(b)** **Controlled status vocabulary** — every status cell starts with one token: `OPEN` / `DONE` / `BLOCKED:<what>` / `WONTFIX`, then any prose. **(c)** **Atomic items** — one item = one deliverable = one status; split genuine multi-deliverable rows into sub-ids (i81→i81a/b, i76→i76a/b…, extending the existing i12a/i48a-b/i74 convention), and *trim* (don't split) rows that are merely over-narrated for a single deliverable — the test: *must you query the parts' completion separately?* yes→split, no→trim. **(d)** Codify the convention in `CLAUDE.md` doc-lifecycle rules and update the ROADMAP handover pointers to the new filenames. **Execution discipline:** calibrate on **i81 + i76** as the worked examples, then **delegate the bulk rewrite to a subagent** (keeps the orchestrator lean — important under the 50% wind-down threshold), and the **pre-merge review MUST explicitly verify id-preservation**: every id present before is present after, none dropped, duplicated, or renumbered (a migration-specific gate beyond the standard §3 checklist). **Benefit beyond tidiness (correctness):** splitting bundled rows surfaces hidden long-tail sub-deliverables as their own `OPEN` rows, so "drained" finally means *actually* drained. | DONE — registry restructure (this PR): open/closed split + controlled status vocabulary + atomic items; see item-registry-open.md / item-registry-closed.md. | `docs/notes/item-registry-open.md`; `docs/notes/question-registry-open.md`; `CLAUDE.md` §doc-lifecycle; `docs/ROADMAP.md` handover protocol; i81, i76 (worked examples); i12a/i48a/i48b/i74 (existing sub-id precedent) |
| **i107a** | **Status-discipline process rule** (the go-forward half of i107, Pete 2026-06-16) — document that a status cell is the controlled-vocabulary token + a short phrase + an optional PR ref, **never** a multi-paragraph narrative of finished sub-results (`Opt-1…Opt-8`, `Brick 1…N`); and that multi-part work is **split into sub-items the moment it's decided multi-part, not at a later audit** (add the sub-items then, give the umbrella a terse status). i105 had the controlled-vocabulary + atomic-items rules but rows drifted into wall-of-text status anyway; this makes the timing + the anti-pattern explicit. | DONE — ✅ PR #347 — strengthened the Controlled-status-vocabulary + Atomic-items sections of `item-registry-open.md` and the matching `CLAUDE.md` doc-lifecycle line; the retroactive sweep of rows that already accreted is **i107b** | `docs/notes/item-registry-open.md` header; `CLAUDE.md` §doc-lifecycle; i105; i107b |
| **i102a** | i102 measurement infra — the cycle-exact T-state counter in the koron-go/z80 harness (`tools/netboot-oracle/z80/tstates.go`, anchor-verified + error-on-unknown completeness) wired into `CallResult.TStates`, plus the recorded crypto baselines (`tstates_baseline_test.go`). The benchmark every later i102 opt measures against. | DONE — ✅ PR #316 | `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/tstates.go`; `tstates_baseline_test.go`; i102 |
| **i102b** | i102 SHA-256 speedups — Opt-1: short-distance rotations (whole-byte `sha_rotr8`/`sha_rotl8` + a minimal bit residual + `sha_shr8`; deleted the bit-at-a-time `sha_rotr_word_by`); Opt-5: dropped the redundant push/pop in `sha_add4` (callers already reload HL/DE). Documented opcodes only; NIST vectors byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #318 (−27.4%) + #322 (−2.7%); SHA-256 ~29.4% below baseline (6.45M→4.55M T) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; i102 |
| **i102c** | i102 quarter-square 8×8 multiply — replaced the `mul8` shift-add loop with the quarter-square identity `a·b = QSQ[a+b] − QSQ[\|a−b\|]` (`QSQ[n]=⌊n²/4⌋`, a 1022-byte RAM table built by `qsq_init` via a pure-add recurrence). Opt-2: x25519; Opt-3: poly1305 (identical loop). Exhaustive `TestMul8Exhaustive`/`TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) + RFC/e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #319 (x25519 −23.0%) + #320 (poly1305 −9.5% / aead −11.5%); later DRY-extracted to `qsq.asm` | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `src/netboot/poly1305.asm`; i102 |
| **i102d** | i102 chacha20 redundant push/pop — `cc_add`/`cc_xor` preserved HL/DE that every `cc_qr` caller reloads; dropping the push/pop also let `cc_addstate`'s manual word-advance go (faster AND smaller). RFC 8439 + aead byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #321 (−7.5% chacha20_block; first i102 increment to shrink code) | `src/netboot/chacha20.asm`; i102 |
| **i102e** | i102 x25519 structural opts — Opt-6: dedicated symmetric squaring `fe_sqr` (496 off-diagonal products doubled + 32 diagonal terms, ~half a multiply); Opt-7: constant-aware `fe_mul121665` (96 vs 1024 mul8 for the 3-byte a24); Opt-8: hold the schoolbook inner-loop pointers in IX/IY across `mul8`. Field pins + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PRs #343 (−18.7%) + #345 (−8.6%) + #346 (−5.64%, −45 B); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→1.125G = −29.9% | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102f** | i102 x25519 Karatsuba field multiply — `fe_schoolbook` (the 32×32→64-byte product behind every `fe_mul`) replaced its flat 1024-`mul8` schoolbook with one level of Karatsuba: split each operand at 16 bytes, three 16×16 sub-multiplies (`kmul16`) for z0/z2/middle (768 `mul8`), the middle coefficient via the subtractive identity `z1 = z0+z2 − (a_lo−a_hi)(b_lo−b_hi)` over absolute differences with a tracked sign bit, then a multi-byte combine (`kadd`/`ksub`). `fe_sqr` left untouched (its symmetric schoolbook already matches Karatsuba's `mul8` count). New `TestFEMulRandom` (1024 random asymmetric pairs) + the existing field pins + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e all byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #354 (x25519 −13.5%); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→0.973G = −39.3% | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/z80/x25519_field_test.go`; i102 |
| **i102g** | i102 `mul8` micro-opts (the 8×8→16 multiply, ~1.66M calls per x25519, shared by x25519 + poly1305) — (1) drop `mul8`'s internal `push de`/`pop hl`: the saved operands `B`/`C` are dead once the difference is taken, so the second `QSQ[\|a−b\|]` lookup reuses `BC` for the table base+entry while `QSQ[a+b]` stays in `DE`; (2) remove the dead `ld d, 0` before every `mul8` call across the x25519 + poly1305 byte-radix loops (`mul8` reads only A and E). `TestMul8Exhaustive` + `TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs each) + RFC 7748 / RFC 8439 / Go e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #355 (x25519 −4.0%, 973.3M→934.1M; also speeds poly1305/aead); x25519 cumulative 1.604G→0.934G = −41.8% | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; `src/netboot/poly1305.asm`; i102 |
| **i102h** | i102 chacha20 register-based rotates — `cc_rotl16`/`cc_rotl8` (the quarter-round's byte-permute rotates, each invoked ~80× per block: ROTL16 once + ROTL8 thrice via ROTL8/ROTL12/ROTL7) read the 4 word bytes into registers A/B/C/D and write them back permuted in place, instead of bouncing them through the `cc_tmp4` memory temp via `cc_load_tmp` (now deleted — faster **and** smaller). RFC 8439 KAT + block/encrypt + AEAD + TLS-client composite byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #356 (chacha20_block −15.5%, 345.5K→292.0K; chacha20_encrypt −15.3%; also speeds aead) | `src/netboot/chacha20.asm`; i102 |
| **i102i** | i102 x25519 schoolbook carry in a register — the multiply/square inner loops (`kmul16`, the Karatsuba 16×16; `fe_sqr` off-diagonal — together ~88% of x25519's `mul8` calls) kept the running byte carry in memory (`fe_car`: a read + a write per iteration). The loops already `push bc`/`pop bc` around `mul8` to preserve the `djnz` counter `B`; riding the carry in `C` through that same push/pop drops both memory accesses for free, and `dest[i+j] + carry` is folded into one `add a,(iy+0)` (the 9-bit sum added to the product via `DE`). math/big field pins + `TestFEMulRandom` + `TestFESqr*` + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #357 (x25519 −6.6%, 934.1M→872.4M) | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102j** | i102 `mul8` page-aligned table index — `qsq_table` is page-aligned (`&C000`/`&FB00`, low byte 0) and the lookup index `2*(a+b)` / `2*\|a−b\|` is ≤ 1020, so each entry address is formed by adding only the high byte (`ld a,qsq_table>>8; add a,h; ld h,a`) instead of a full `ld de,qsq_table; add hl,de` — the index's low byte is already the address low byte. Saves ~12 T per `mul8` call (~1.77M calls per x25519). Invariant documented at the `qsq_table` equ and guarded by the exhaustive `TestMul8Exhaustive`/`TestPoly1305Mul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) — any non-page-aligned base fails CI. | DONE — ✅ PR #358 (x25519 −2.7%, 872.4M→848.7M; also speeds poly1305) | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; i102 |
| **i102k** | i102 reduction/constant-multiply carry in a register — applied the i102i carry-in-`C` pattern to the two remaining byte-radix multiply loops: `fe_mul38_high` (the `×38` high-half fold, run on **every** fe_mul **and** fe_sqr reduction) and `fe_121665_inner` (the `×a24` curve-constant multiply). Both kept the running carry in `fe_car` memory; now it rides the existing `push bc`/`pop bc` around `mul8` in `C`, and the addend is folded into one `add` (`fe_m38` has only the carry; `fe_121665` folds dest+carry via `add a,(iy+0)` like `kmul16`). Completes the carry-register conversion across all byte-radix multiply loops, so the `fe_car` scratch byte is now fully dead and is removed (its last users dropped it in i102i/this PR). Field pins + `TestFEMul121665` + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #359 (x25519 −0.5%, 848.7M→844.5M) | `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102l** | i102 `mul8` inlined at the two hottest x25519 sites — the quarter-square `mul8` body (the 8×8→16 multiply, ~1.8M calls per x25519) was a called subroutine paying `call`+`ret` (27 T) per invocation. Refactored into a pyz80 `mul8_body` MACRO (single source-of-truth; the i102c qsq DRY extraction is preserved — `mul8` is now `mul8_body` + `ret`) and expanded INLINE at `kmul16_inner` (the Karatsuba 16×16) and `fe_sqr_inner` (the off-diagonal squarer), ~96% of the calls. The branch over `neg` uses `jr nc,$+4` (skip the 2-byte `neg`) so the macro carries no internal label and expands any number of times. `fe_sqr_diag` + poly1305 + the host field tests keep calling the subroutine. `TestMul8Exhaustive` (all 65536 pairs) + RFC 7748 + Go-curve25519 e2e + the TLS-client composite headroom assertion (`tls_client_end < qsq_table`, ~1.1 KB clearance after ~70 B growth) byte-exact. | DONE — ✅ PR #363 (x25519 −5.77%, 844.5M→795.8M) | `src/netboot/qsq.asm`; `src/netboot/x25519.asm`; i102 |
| **i102n** | i102 **sha256 register-oriented rewrite — increment 1** (resolves q21: do the rewrite, keep cryptographic SHA-256). Replaced `sha256_compress` (memory-to-memory, ~2.5–3k `call`/`ret` per block) with a register-resident design: inline `add4`/`copy4` macros; the σ/Σ rotates held in B/C/D/E (8T `rr r` + free whole-byte relabels, not 15T `rr (hl)`) — the big lever; simplified `Ch=g^(e&(f^g))` / `Maj=(a&b)|(c&(a^b))` (no `CPL`); chained rotations (load once, snapshot each term); in-place W schedule; `add4` on LSB pointers; dead subroutines removed. ABI byte-preserved (`sha256_init`/`update`/`final` + `SHA_H`/`SHA_BITLEN`/`SHA_FILL`/`SHA_BLOCK`). **729,795 → 418,843 T/block (−42.6%, 1.74×)** — the **fastest verified Z80 SHA-256** (vs timewave0/ZARC ~480–485k; oisee's "45k" is fabricated AI code that doesn't assemble/run/hash). Byte-exact: NIST KAT (incl. the million-'a' long vector) + the real `start.elf` (2.98 MB) → `dd9b4204…ec84` + full `ci-netboot-z80`; TLS composite fits (`tls_client_end` &F7BD < &FB00, 835 B). The 8× unroll/circular-renaming (~9% more) was correct-in-isolation but reverted on a pyz80 `EQU FOR` rewind fragility — follow-up under i102m. | DONE — ✅ PR #365 (sha256 −42.6%, 729.8K→418.8K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; i102m, i100, i101; q21 |
| **i102o** | i102 **sha256 8× circular-renaming unroll — increment 2**. Unrolls the 64 rounds into an 8-phase group looped 8×: with circular renaming a..h never physically move, so the per-round 28-byte `lddr` shuffle (~37k T/block of data movement) is gone — each phase hard-codes which `wv_` slot is a..h. **418,843 → 377,371 T/block (−9.9%)**; cumulative sha256 729,795→377,371 (−48.3%). Generated **flat** (ordinary mnemonics, no pyz80 `EQU FOR`/macros — sidesteps the `EQU FOR`×macro rewind bug that reverted the first attempt) by `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/gen_sha256_unrolled.py`; the committed inline block is **drift-guarded byte-for-byte** by `regen_guard_test.go` (in `ci-netboot-z80`). **Gated** behind `if defined(NETBOOT_TLS_CLIENT)` — the TLS composite keeps the smaller rolled loop (`tls_client_end` &F7BD < &FB00 unchanged); only the standalone/firmware build unrolls (~2.4 KB). Pre-req: `sha_ch`/`sha_maj` take caller-set source pointers (both paths). Byte-exact: NIST KAT + million-'a' + the real `start.elf` (2.98 MB) → `dd9b4204…ec84` + full `ci-netboot-z80`; `TestSHA256PerBlockCompress` pins ≤418,843. | DONE — ✅ PR #367 (sha256 −9.9%, 418.8K→377.4K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; i102m, i102n, i100, i101 |
| **i102p** | i102 **sha256 inline Ch/Maj — increment 3**. In the unrolled (firmware/standalone) round path the operand slots for `Ch(e,f,g)` and `Maj(a,b,c)` are compile-time constants, so the 8 generated phases now inline both instead of calling the shared `sha_ch`/`sha_maj` subroutines. The subroutines take their three source pointers at runtime and so pay a **19 T `(ix+d)`/`(iy+d)` indexed access per operand byte** plus `call`/`djnz` overhead; inline, each operand loads with a plain `ld a,(nnnn)` and the boolean is done in A with H,L,D as scratch (`Ch` 76 T/byte vs ~135; `Maj` 92 T/byte vs ~141). **377,371 → 346,267 T/block (−8.2%)**; cumulative sha256 729,795→346,267 (−52.6%). Emitted by `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/gen_sha256_unrolled.py` (`ch_inline`/`maj_inline`), drift-guarded byte-for-byte by `regen_guard_test.go`. The shared subroutines are untouched — the rolled `NETBOOT_TLS_CLIENT` composite still calls them, so its binary is byte-identical (built green, budget unchanged). Byte-exact: full NIST KAT + the million-'a' long vector (15,625 blocks through the unrolled compress) + full `ci-netboot-z80`; `TestSHA256PerBlockCompress` now pins ≤346,267. | DONE — ✅ PR #369 (sha256 −8.2%, 377.4K→346.3K T/block) | `src/netboot/sha256.asm`; `tools/sha256-unroll-gen/`; i102m, i102n, i102o, i100, i101 |
| **i100a** | i100 http_main host slice — Go-authority `http.Provisioner` (PR #307) + Z80 `http_main` Bricks 1–6: Brick 1 compose + plan (PR #308), Brick 2 dynamic `HTTP_PATH_PTR` (PR #309), Brick 3 bodySink header-skip seam (PR #310), Brick 4 `prov_start` per-file re-init (PR #311), Brick 5 per-file store double `store_begin`/`store_end` (PR #313), Brick 6 `prov_first`/`prov_onframe`/`prov_next` download loop (PR #314); every frame + verdict host-verified byte-for-byte vs Go `Provisioner`; bootable image byte-identical throughout (Brick 7 deferred to hardware, tracked in i100). | DONE — ✅ PRs #307–#314 + tracking PR #315 | `tools/netboot-oracle/http/provision.go`; `src/netboot/http_main.asm`; `src/netboot/fw_source.asm`; i100 (open umbrella) |
| **i100b** | i100 **Brick 7 — bootable migration + real B-DOS HSAVE-per-record store leaf** (the final brick of the http_main port plan). Un-guarded the streaming sink + SHA-256 verify + sha256 into the real bootable (`NETBOOT_STREAM`, sharing the host harness's host-verified flush/verify code), and added the real `storage_sink_leaf` / `store_begin` / `store_end` in `http_main.asm` — each flushed `FW_RECORD_CAP`-byte window hashed into the running verify then HSAVE'd to Trinity as record `<prefix><NNN>` (`fw_span_record_name` + `bdos_seam` UIFA + `RST 8` HSAVE). Deleted the legacy single-file bootable `http_main` + its 16 KB `STAGING` from `netboot_http.asm`. Bootable ends &ECED (27885 B, 4883 B free under &10000); Makefile fit-check added (pyz80 doesn't catch org overrun). **Record cap = provisional 4096** (q22 pins the real value on hardware; chosen so start.elf ~2.98 MB fits the 1000-record bound). Also fixed the boot entry: the bootable now `jp http_main` at &8000 (was the included `tcp_connect`) — the same latent bug exists in the sibling netboot bootables, tracked as **i110**. The HSAVE store leaf + EEPROM read are **non-host-verifiable** (q16) and ship **unverified until Pete's Trinity test** (CLAUDE.md §5); the wire/fetch/verify loop stays host-verified (`TestProvision*Z80` green). | DONE — ✅ PR #373 | `src/netboot/http_main.asm`; `src/netboot/tcp_conn.asm`; `src/netboot/netboot_http.asm`; `src/netboot/fw_span.asm`; `tools/netboot-oracle/bdos/span.go`; i100 (umbrella), i100a; i110 (sibling entry-point fix); q16, q22 |
